@chapter Overview of flexichain
@cindex Overview of flexichain

flexichain is a flexible sequence data structure for Bigloo scheme, ported from the original Common Lisp implementation by Robert Strandh and Matthieu Villeneuve. It provides efficient insertion and deletion operations at arbitrary positions in a sequence, making it ideal for applications like text editors, syntax analyzers, and other scenarios requiring frequent sequence modifications.

The key advantages of flexichain over traditional vectors or lists are:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Efficient insertion/deletion}: O(1) amortized time for insertions and deletions at arbitrary positions
@item
@strong{Gap buffer implementation}: Uses a gap buffer internally for optimal performance
@item
@strong{Cursor support}: Provides cursors that maintain their position even as the sequence is modified
@item
@strong{Ranked elements}: Optional support for elements that know their position in the sequence
@item
@strong{Type safety}: Full integration with Bigloo's type system and comprehensive error handling
@end itemize

@section Basic Usage

@lisp
(module example-basic-usage (library flexichain))

;; Create a new flexichain
(define chain (make-gapbuffer))

;; Insert elements
(flexi-insert! chain 0 "hello")
(flexi-insert! chain 1 "world")

;; Access elements
(flexi-ref chain 0)  ; => "hello"
(flexi-length chain) ; => 2

;; Delete elements
(flexi-delete! chain 0)
@end lisp

